This suite checks that ganesha recalls NFSv4 delegations when a CephFS
client other than ganesha accesses the same file. An NFS client (kernel
mount of the ganesha export) holds a file open under a delegation while
ceph-fuse writes, reads or chmods it. The MDS revokes caps from ganesha's
libcephfs client, which calls back into ganesha (FSAL_CEPH) to recall the
delegation. The ganesha-deleg-recall task checks that the NFS client returns
its delegation and that both clients see the same data.

Scenarios that hit a known ganesha bug are listed under known_failures in
3-deleg-recall.yaml. They are logged, not failed; the task logs any known
failure that passed. Both bugs below need fixing for every scenario to pass.

  read-deleg-vs-write read-deleg-vs-setattr
    The delegation is not recalled once the NFS client has closed the open
    that obtained it. FSAL_CEPH sets the libcephfs delegation on the Fh of
    that open and releases the Fh at CLOSE, which drops the libcephfs
    delegation. The NFS client keeps its delegation after CLOSE (as NFSv4
    allows) and ganesha's state layer still records it, but nothing ties it
    to CephFS any more: a conflicting CephFS client gets its caps without a
    recall. The NFS client keeps serving cached (or, with a write delegation,
    unflushed) data. The DELEGRETURN at unmount logs
    "fsal_start_io :CRIT :No openstate with delegation".

    Both scenarios create the file over NFS, close it and reopen it, so the
    reopen runs under the delegation from the create.

  write-deleg-vs-read
    The NFS client holds the creating open, so the libcephfs delegation is
    in place and the recall is sent, but too late. FSAL_CEPH's recall
    callback (ceph_deleg_cb) queues the CB_RECALL and at once releases the
    libcephfs delegation (ceph_join_deleg ->
    ceph_ll_delegation(CEPH_DELEGATION_NONE)), instead of when the NFS client
    returns the delegation (DELEGRETURN). libcephfs drops the caps the
    delegation held, the MDS completes the revoke and the CephFS client goes
    ahead before the NFS client has flushed: the CephFS read misses the NFS
    client's buffered write. Introduced by ganesha commit 271aa38243cb
    ("FSAL_CEPH: Avoid OPEN_DOWNGRADE hang by fixing async deleg recall
    handling"); every recall triggered by another CephFS client is affected.

Conflicts between NFS clients of the same ganesha are caught in ganesha's
state layer and are not affected by either bug (pynfs does not see them).
Any other CephFS client is: the kernel client, ceph-fuse, other libcephfs
users, and other ganesha daemons exporting the same file system (an NFS
cluster with more than one backend). Fixing them is part of supporting
multi-protocol access and multiple ganesha servers exporting the same share.
When a fix lands, drop the scenarios it fixes from known_failures.
